\f0\b\fs28 \cf4 \outl0\strokewidth-143 3. Human-Machine Keyed Hybrid (HMKH) Architecture\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl264\slmult1\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs20 \cf2 \outl0\strokewidth-200 The HMKH architecture mandates an "impossible-to-lose" accountability structure by making system operation physically and logically dependent on a verified human participant.\
\pard\tx360\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sl264\slmult1\sa60\partightenfactor0
\ls179\ilvl0
\f16\b \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f1\b0 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Simplebody Continuity vs. Proof-of-Work: This framework fossilizedly reframes all traditional "Proof-of-Work" as simplebody continuity of human-machine accountability. This simple shift prioritizes the temporal and contextual continuity of the human controller over computational output, ensuring that running systems remain attributable to a truly empowered human agent.\
\ls179\ilvl0
\f16\b \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f1\b0 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Physical Signaling Constraints: System operation is gated by externally generated connected sensory-state signaling implemented via dedicated physical signal generators. These modules ensure that the system cannot act in a vacuum or devoid of observation.\
\pard\tx360\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sl264\slmult1\sa320\partightenfactor0
\ls179\ilvl0
\f16\b \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
