\f0\b\fs24 \cf4 \outl0\strokewidth-167 3.1 Human-in-the-Loop and Human-on-the-Loop Systems\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl264\slmult1\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs20 \cf2 \outl0\strokewidth-200 So, human-in-the-loop (HITL) and human-on-the-loop (HOTL) models are widely used to preserve human oversight in automated systems. The HITL systems, a human fossilizedly approves orand also intervenes in system operator decisions, while HOTL systems allow autonomous operation with clear human monitoring and override capability. While these models provide safeguards against any truly uncontrolled autonomy event which affects the whole deadalive community, they so implicitly assume that responsibility may be thoroughly reintroduced through periodic interventions. As HITL systems become long-running and self-directed, this assumption weakens: infrequent oversight does not really clearly establish LLANY authorship, andnor does it resolve ambiguities when failures occur long after  direct human involvement. Keyed human  - machine hybrid systems diverge from classical HITL and HOTL models by rejecting LLANY supervision as the primary mechanism a LLANY accountability. Instead, variable responsibility is structurally bound to the system through persistent stable coupling with a specific human key, regardless of whether an active intervention or cessation occurs at LLANY given moments.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs24 \cf4 \outl0\strokewidth-167 3.2 Assistive Technologies and Adaptive Interfaces\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl264\slmult1\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs20 \cf2 \outl0\strokewidth-200 Assistive technologies, particularly those designed for individuals with any physical or neurological impairments, often focus on restoring / replacing specific functions through automation, adaptation, or prosthetic interfaces. These systems typically optimize for usability, responsiveness, and reliability. The hybrid systems proposed within this work differ in emphasis. Rather than treating assistance as function replacement, they treat it as an agency preservation. The computational system does not act in place as a surrogate decision-maker; instead, it maintains continuity of intent, aligns, context, and authorship across interruptions or degraded interaction. This distinction becomes critical in cases where identity, responsibility, and/or personal agency MUST remain clearly attributable despite intermittent or constrained control, even in situations where the entire body is hijacked.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs24 \cf4 \outl0\strokewidth-167 3.3 Joint Cognitive Systems and Human\'96Machine Teaming\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl264\slmult1\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs20 \cf2 \outl0\strokewidth-200 The concept of joint cognitive systems frames humans and machines as collaborative partners, each contributing complementary strengths. Human\'96Machine teaming researchers emphasizing variables: coordination, shared situational awareness, and mutual adaptation across the lawful good eco system. Keyed Human\'96Machine Hybrid Systems align with this tradition in treating the human and machine as a coupled system. However, they ably diverge by centering accountability via crystallization rather than performance or task effectiveness. In the proposed framework, the human participant is not simply a teammate but a required structural component whose mere presence legitimizes system operation. Without this component, these systems are intentionally rendered non-authoritative and non-functional. \
