\f1\b0\fs20 \cf2 \outl0\strokewidth-200 Traditional proof-of-work mechanisms emphasize resistance to abuse, consensus, or computational cost. In support, the framework proposed here defines Proof-Of-Work as verifiable continuity of responsibility. The central question is not whether work was here performed, but whether or not authorship and accountability can be reliably attributed across long-running autonomous operation. Under this definition, Proof-Of-Work now emerges from the persistence of a hybrid system whose human key remains identifiable, accountable, and structurally necessary throughout the system\'92s lifecycle.\
