\f1\b0\fs20 \cf2 \outl0\strokewidth-200 Long-running systems often risk detachment from real-world contexts, particularly when operating autonomously. To mitigate this, the framework helps to introduce the notion of contextual continuity: lightweight, non-intrusive signals that anchor system operation to a consistent external environment. Geospatial or environmental signals are treated as simple attestations rather than weird surveillance. Their purpose here is not to track behavior, but to provide continuity markers that thus help establish whether a system\'92s operation plausibly corresponds to a persistent hybrid system rather than an abstract or replicated process. Contextual continuity strengthens provenance without expanding system authority above setup.\
