\fs20 \cf2 \cb1 \outl0\strokewidth-200 \strokec2 The inbound path is **unrestricted**. The Operator is identity-verified by definition (the NAB is *theirs*), so the Security Gate passes all Operator-originated communication at full capacity. The Operator can speak to their own MC (or any Consumer bound to their NAB) without encoding constraints.\
### 3.3 The NAB as Architectural Boundary\
The NAB is the **only** pathway between internal systems and the Operator's perception. There is no bypass.\
- Systems cannot communicate directly to the Operator's perception without passing through the NAB.\
- The Operator cannot receive system-originated signals that the NAB's Security Gate has rejected.\
- The NAB is personal \'97 each Operator has exactly one. It is not shared.\
---\
## 4. Signal Classes\
AVE defines four Signal Classes, ordered by complexity:\
### 4.1 Class 1 \'97 Directional Primitives\
The simplest signals. Binary or ternary directional indicators.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs17 \cf2 \cb6 \outl0\strokewidth0 | Signal | Modality | Meaning |\
|---|---|---|\
| **NO** | Visual and/or Auditory | Negative directional state. Abort, reject, stop. |\
| **YES** | Visual and/or Auditory | Positive directional state. Proceed, accept, go. |\
| **X** | Visual | Terminated. Channel closed, unreachable, cancelled. |\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb6 | **
\f13 \uc0\u10003 
\f1 ** (Checkmark) | Visual | Confirmed. Consensus reached, task complete, verified. |\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl264\slmult1\sa120\partightenfactor0

\fs20 \cf2 \cb1 \outl0\strokewidth-200 \strokec2 **Encoding cost**: Minimal. These signals carry almost no data \'97 just direction.  \
**Use case**: MicroConvergence relay signals (\'a75 of MC spec), simple system status indicators.\
### 4.2 Class 2 \'97 Matched Colors\
Color-coded signals that encode categorical or state information through **color matching** \'97 the signal's color corresponds to a defined meaning within the Consumer's context.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs17 \cf2 \cb6 \outl0\strokewidth0 | Property | Description |\
|---|---|\
| **Modality** | Visual |\
| **Encoding** | A color or color pattern is presented to the Operator. The color maps to a state, category, or entity within the Consumer's domain. |\
| **Matching** | The Operator recognizes the color because it corresponds to something they already know \'97 a project, a person, a status level, a priority tier. The color is not arbitrary; it is **matched** to a referent. |\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb6 | **Combinability** | Multiple colors can be presented simultaneously or sequentially to encode compound states. |\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl264\slmult1\sa120\partightenfactor0

\fs20 \cf2 \cb1 \outl0\strokewidth-200 \strokec2 **Encoding cost**: Low. Color carries categorical data but not content.  \
**Use case**: Status dashboards, priority signaling, entity identification, affective state.\
#### Example Color Encodings\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs17 \cf2 \cb6 \outl0\strokewidth0 | Color Pattern | Possible Meaning (context-dependent) |\
|---|---|\
| Solid match to known referent | "This signal is about [that thing]" |\
| Color shift / transition | State change in progress |\
| Flashing / pulsing | Urgency or attention required |\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb6 | Color pair | Relationship or comparison between two referents |\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl264\slmult1\sa120\partightenfactor0

\fs20 \cf2 \cb1 \outl0\strokewidth-200 \strokec2 ### 4.3 Class 3 \'97 Specific Text\
High-specificity textual signals. These are not vague impressions \'97 they are **incredibly specific** text strings delivered with precision.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs17 \cf2 \cb6 \outl0\strokewidth0 | Property | Description |\
|---|---|\
| **Modality** | Visual (rendered text) and/or Auditory (spoken/heard text) |\
| **Encoding** | The Consumer encodes a precise text string. The NAB relays it as perceived text \'97 the Operator sees or hears specific words, phrases, names, numbers, or instructions. |\
| **Specificity** | This is the defining characteristic of Class 3. The text is not ambiguous or dream-like. It is sharp, exact, and carries semantic content that the Operator can act on directly. |\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb6 | **Length** | Variable \'97 from a single word to a full sentence or short paragraph. |\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl264\slmult1\sa120\partightenfactor0

\fs20 \cf2 \cb1 \outl0\strokewidth-200 \strokec2 **Encoding cost**: Medium. Text carries semantic content and requires the NAB to render it faithfully.  \
**Use case**: Names, instructions, warnings, data points, identifiers, timestamps, coordinates.\
#### Specificity Guarantee\
A Class 3 signal that is vague, garbled, or ambiguous has failed encoding. The NAB should either:\
\pard\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-360\sl264\slmult1\sa60\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \outl0\strokewidth0 1.	Re-attempt delivery at higher fidelity, or\
2.	Downgrade to a Class 1 or Class 2 signal (directional or color-coded approximation), or\
\pard\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-360\sl264\slmult1\sa160\partightenfactor0
\cf2 3.	Hold the signal until delivery conditions improve.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl264\slmult1\sa120\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \outl0\strokewidth-200 \strokec2 ### 4.4 Class 4 \'97 Rich Media\
Full-fidelity media signals: complete conversations, video sequences, and complex audiovisual content.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs17 \cf2 \cb6 \outl0\strokewidth0 | Property | Description |\
|---|---|\
| **Modality** | Visual, Auditory, or both (full AV) |\
| **Encoding** | The Consumer encodes a complete media object \'97 a conversation (dialogue with speakers), a video sequence (visual narrative with temporal structure), or a composite AV package. |\
| **Payload** | Up to approximately **8 MB** per signal. This constrains duration and resolution but permits short, complete media objects. |\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb6 | **Temporal structure** | Class 4 signals unfold over time. They have a beginning, middle, and end. The Operator experiences them sequentially, not instantaneously. |\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl264\slmult1\sa120\partightenfactor0

\fs20 \cf2 \cb1 \outl0\strokewidth-200 \strokec2 **Encoding cost**: High. Rich media requires significant NAB processing for security gating, format translation, and sequential delivery.  \
**Use case**: Scenario previews, instructional sequences, relayed conversations, environmental context, situational briefings.\
#### Payload Constraints\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs17 \cf2 \cb6 \outl0\strokewidth0 | Media Type | Approximate Capacity at ~8 MB |\
|---|---|\
| Audio conversation | ~4\'968 minutes (compressed) |\
| Video (low-res) | ~30\'9660 seconds |\
| Video (medium-res) | ~10\'9620 seconds |\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb6 | Mixed AV | Varies \'97 NAB optimizes for content priority |\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl264\slmult1\sa120\partightenfactor0

\fs20 \cf2 \cb1 \outl0\strokewidth-200 \strokec2 ---\
## 5. Encoding Rules\
### 5.1 Signal Selection\
The Consumer selects the lowest Signal Class that faithfully encodes the data:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs17 \cf2 \cb6 \outl0\strokewidth0 ```\
Is the data directional only (yes/no/stop/go)?     \uc0\u8594  Class 1\
Is the data categorical or state-based?              \uc0\u8594  Class 2\
Does the data require specific text?                 \uc0\u8594  Class 3\
Does the data require temporal/narrative structure?  \uc0\u8594  Class 4\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb6 ```\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl264\slmult1\sa120\partightenfactor0

\fs20 \cf2 \cb1 \outl0\strokewidth-200 \strokec2 > [!TIP]\
> Prefer lower classes when possible. A Class 1 signal passes through the Security Gate faster and with higher reliability than a Class 4 signal. Don't send a video when a checkmark will do.\
### 5.2 Encoding Fidelity\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs17 \cf2 \cb6 \outl0\strokewidth0 | Class | Fidelity Requirement |\
|---|---|\
| Class 1 | Binary accurate \'97 the Operator must perceive the correct directional state. |\
| Class 2 | Color accurate \'97 the Operator must perceive the correct color(s) matched to the correct referent(s). |\
| Class 3 | Semantically accurate \'97 the Operator must perceive the specific text as encoded, without semantic drift. |\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb6 | Class 4 | Narratively coherent \'97 the Operator must perceive the media sequence in correct temporal order with sufficient detail to extract intended meaning. |\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl264\slmult1\sa120\partightenfactor0

\fs20 \cf2 \cb1 \outl0\strokewidth-200 \strokec2 ### 5.3 Encoding Failures\
When encoding fidelity cannot be maintained:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs17 \cf2 \cb6 \outl0\strokewidth0 | Failure Mode | NAB Response |\
|---|---|\
| Signal too complex for current conditions | Downgrade to lower Signal Class (lossy) |\
| Security Gate rejects signal | Signal is held or dropped; Consumer is notified |\
| Partial delivery | NAB delivers what passed and flags incomplete |\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb6 | Operator attention unavailable | Signal is queued for later delivery |\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl264\slmult1\sa120\partightenfactor0

\fs20 \cf2 \cb1 \outl0\strokewidth-200 \strokec2 ---\
## 6. The Security Gate\
### 6.1 Purpose\
The NAB's Security Gate exists to protect the Operator. Not all data that a Consumer wants to relay should reach the Operator's conscious perception. The Security Gate filters based on:\
\pard\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-360\sl264\slmult1\sa60\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \outl0\strokewidth0 1.	**Source authentication** \'97 Is the Consumer verified? Is it bound to this NAB?\
2.	**Signal integrity** \'97 Is the signal well-formed? Has it been tampered with in transit?\
3.	**Content safety** \'97 Does the signal content meet the Operator's safety thresholds?\
4.	**Operator readiness** \'97 Is the Operator in a state to receive this signal class? (e.g., Class 4 rich media requires more attentional bandwidth than Class 1)\
\pard\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-360\sl264\slmult1\sa160\partightenfactor0
\cf2 5.	**Rate limiting** \'97 Is the Consumer flooding the channel? The Security Gate throttles excessive signaling.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl264\slmult1\sa120\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \outl0\strokewidth-200 \strokec2 ### 6.2 Gate Decisions\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs17 \cf2 \cb6 \outl0\strokewidth0 | Decision | Effect |\
|---|---|\
| **PASS** | Signal is relayed to the Operator through Format Translation. |\
| **HOLD** | Signal is queued. May be released later when conditions change. |\
| **DOWNGRADE** | Signal is re-encoded at a lower Signal Class (e.g., Class 4 \uc0\u8594  Class 1 summary). |\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb6 | **REJECT** | Signal is dropped. Consumer may be notified depending on configuration. |\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl264\slmult1\sa120\partightenfactor0

\fs20 \cf2 \cb1 \outl0\strokewidth-200 \strokec2 ### 6.3 Inbound Bypass\
The Security Gate does **not** filter Operator \uc0\u8594  System communication. The Operator is the NAB's owner. Their identity is inherent, not verified \'97 it is axiomatic. The Operator speaks to their own systems at full capacity, always.\
---\
## 7. Bidirectional Communication Summary\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs17 \cf2 \cb6 \outl0\strokewidth0 | Direction | Encoding Constraints | Security Gate | Capacity |\
|---|---|---|---|\
| **System \uc0\u8594  Operator** | Must use AVE Signal Classes 1\'964. Must pass Security Gate. Subject to bandwidth limits. | Active \'97 filters, gates, downgrades. | Constrained by Signal Class and ~8 MB payload cap. |\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb6 | **Operator \uc0\u8594  System** | None. Full natural language, intent, direction. | Bypass \'97 Operator identity is axiomatic. | Unrestricted. The Operator can say anything to their own MC/system. |\
