\f0\b\fs24 \cf4 \outl0\strokewidth-167 The Sugar Rush Sabotage\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl264\slmult1\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs20 \cf2 \outl0\strokewidth-200 Sarah Chen stared at the lab results, her third cup of tea of the morning growing cold on her desk. Two weeks after the coffee bean incident, a new anomaly had surfaced. \
"It's not just a localized spike," Marcus said, leaning over her shoulder. "Every major soft drink manufacturer on the West Coast is reporting the same thing. Their automated sweetness sensors are completely out of whack."\
"Out of whack how?" Sarah asked.\
"They're reading normal sucrose levels, but the actual sugar content is nearly triple what it should be. If this goes out to the public, we're looking at a massive public health crisis. Not to mention the liability."\
Sarah rubbed her eyes. "The sensors were calibrated last week. How could they all be wrong at the same time?"\
"Because," Marcus pulled up a schematic on his tablet, "they all use the same optical refraction firmware. The same firmware that was updated remotely three days ago by a third-party vendor."\
Sarah felt a familiar chill. "Let me guess. The vendor is a subsidiary of the same shell company that supplied the counterfeit freezer chips."\
"Bingo," Marcus said. "They aren't trying to shut down the food supply this time. They're trying to alter it from the inside."\
Sarah stood up, grabbing her coat. "Get the manufacturers to halt the bottling lines immediately. We need to find out who authorized that firmware update before this gets any sweeter."\
As they headed for the elevators, Marcus sighed. "First onions, then coffee, now sugar. What's next? Water?"\
"Don't give them any ideas," Sarah replied. "Let's just hope we can catch them before they ruin dessert."\page September 20th, 2026 | Harshal Priyavadan Patel | Google Antigravity Gemini 3.8 Flash High |\
